On solid substrates exposed to air, biofilms often display quasi-planar morphologies that evolve into buckled and wrinkled surfaces 11 . Lateral expansion against a rigid, non-growing substrate generates in-plane compressive stress; when this stress exceeds a critical threshold, the structure buckles, leading to characteristic patterns such as wrinkling, folding, or delamination 43 . To capture the mechanical mechanisms underpinning pattern formation in biofilms, thin film buckling theory was employed to reveal radial wrinkling and localized blistering resulting from strain mismatches (Fig.  3a ) 44 . In this framework, the biofilm–substrate system is typically modeled as a bi- or tri-layer structure with distinct mechanical properties, where nonuniform growth coupled with local nutrient availability, stiffness contrast, interfacial adhesion, and friction collectively govern stress anisotropy and mechanical instabilities, leading to diverse wrinkling patterns 45 , 46 . A 2D chemical mechanics model reveals that the biofilm center and edge experience different nutrient availability, which impacts the local stress state via radial expansion rate 11 . Faster expansion velocity of nutrient-rich region at biofilm edge increases the radial stress, while biofilm-surface friction contributes to circumferential stress distribution and stress isotropy 11 . Under high anisotropy stress, wrinkle direction tends to be perpendicular to maximum compressive stress, while zigzag herringbone-like patterns appear under isotropic stress 11 . Localized cell death related to nutrient consumption can further concentrate mechanical stresses and trigger vertical buckling, further driving wrinkle formation in biofilms 47 . In this process, the EPS matrix plays an important role in maintaining a connective and elastic pellicle 46 , 48 . A mutant lacking regular EPS secretion has no surface features 44 . Fig. 3 Mechanisms of biofilm 2D-to-3D structural evolution under different environmental c