A more complex mechanical environment is imposed by dynamic fluid shear. Biofilms growing on solid-fluid surface under high shear flow usually develop a more compact droplet shape with horizontal cell orientation in the downstream direction 57 . Other than bulk deformation or erosion of biomass, fluid shear also shapes biofilm structural developments through its effects on matrix composition and individual cell dynamics, given the shear-induced erosion can be resisted by cell-cell adhesion at shear rates up to  γ ̇ = 2000 s − 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
